       <div class="faq-q" role="button" tabindex="0">Does Emergency SOS work in Kenya? What number does it call?</div>
       <div class="faq-a">Emergency SOS calls the emergency services number for your region. In Kenya this is 999 (Police) or 112 (general emergency, works on most networks including Safaricom, Airtel, and Telkom). The phone also shares your location with your chosen emergency contacts.</div>
     </div>
     <div class="faq-item">
       <div class="faq-q" role="button" tabindex="0">My phone is a Tecno or Infinix. Do these settings still apply?</div>
       <div class="faq-a">Yes. Tecno and Infinix run Android, so the general steps are the same. Exact menu names may vary slightly. If you cannot find a setting, ask a family member to help search for it — every Android phone has a search bar at the top of the Settings menu.</div>
     </div>
     <div class="faq-item">
       <div class="faq-q" role="button" tabindex="0">How do I use voice typing in Kiswahili?</div>
       <div class="faq-a">Most Android phones support Kiswahili voice input through Google's keyboard (Gboard), available free on the Google Play Store. Go to Gboard Settings → Languages → Add Kiswahili. Once added, tap the microphone and speak in Kiswahili — your phone will type it.</div>
     </div>
     <div class="faq-item">
       <div class="faq-q" role="button" tabindex="0">What if I accidentally activate Emergency SOS?</div>
       <div class="faq-a">Most phones give you a 3–5 second countdown before placing the call. You will see a Cancel button on the screen. Tap it before the countdown ends and no call will be made.</div>
     </div>
     <div class="faq-item">
       <div class="faq-q" role="button" tabindex="0">Can my grandchild help me set these up remotely?</div>
       <div class="faq-a">Yes. Share this article with them on WhatsApp and ask them to walk you through the steps on a voice or video call. It typically takes less than 15 minutes to set up all five settings.</div>
     </div>
